\description{
MEXGB is an adaptation of extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost, Chen and Guestrin, 2016)
<doi:10.1145/2939672.2939785> to longitudinal data. It follows exactly the same
Expectation-Maximization (EM) / Best Linear Unbiased Prediction (BLUP) scheme as the
Mixed Effect Gradient Boosting (MEGB) algorithm of the R package \code{MEGB}, which in turn
follows the Mixed Effect Random Forest (MERF) of Hajjem et. al. (2014)
<doi:10.1080/00949655.2012.741599> as implemented by Capitaine et. al. (2021)
<doi:10.1177/0962280220946080>. The only change with respect to MEGB is that the
fixed-effect function is fitted with the regularised second-order (Newton) tree boosting
of the \code{xgboost} package instead of the first-order boosting of the \code{gbm} package,
and that binary outcomes are handled by penalised quasi-likelihood (PQL).
}
\details{
For a continuous (\code{family = "gaussian"}) outcome the algorithm estimates the parameters of
the semi-parametric mixed-effects model: \deqn{Y_i(t)=f(X_i(t))+Z_i(t)\beta_i+\epsilon_i}
with \eqn{Y_i(t)} the output at time \eqn{t} for the \eqn{i}th individual; \eqn{X_i(t)} the input
predictors (fixed effects) at time \eqn{t} for the \eqn{i}th individual; \eqn{Z_i(t)} are the
random effects at time \eqn{t} for the \eqn{i}th individual; \eqn{\epsilon_i} is the residual error.

For a binary (\code{family = "binomial"}) outcome the model is
\deqn{logit P(Y_i(t)=1 | \beta_i)=f(X_i(t))+Z_i(t)\beta_i.}
The EM loop is identical to the continuous algorithm with two changes. (i) In the M-step,
\eqn{f} is fitted by logistic extreme gradient boosting (\code{objective = "binary:logistic"},
i.e. Newton steps with gradient \eqn{\mu-y} and Hessian \eqn{\mu(1-\mu)}) with the current
random-effect contribution \eqn{Z_i\hat\beta_i} supplied as an offset (\code{base_margin}),
the logit-scale analogue of the partial residual \eqn{Y-Z\hat\beta} used for continuous
outcomes. (ii) In the E-step the model is linearised at the current fit by penalised
quasi-likelihood (PQL): with \eqn{\mu=expit(\eta)} and \eqn{w=\mu(1-\mu)}, the working
response \eqn{\tilde y=\eta+(y-\mu)/w} replaces \eqn{Y} and the residual covariance
\eqn{\sigma^2 I} is replaced by \eqn{W^{-1}=diag(1/w)} in the BLUP and variance updates.

For a time-to-event (\code{family = "cox"}) outcome the model is the mixed-effects (log-normal
frailty) Cox model \deqn{\lambda_i(t | X_i, \beta_i)=\lambda_0(t)\exp\{f(X_i)+Z_i\beta_i\}.}
The M-step fits \eqn{f} by \code{xgboost} with \code{objective = "survival:cox"} (Newton steps on
the Cox partial likelihood) and the offset \eqn{Z_i\hat\beta_i}; the E-step is the PQL BLUP of the
Poisson representation of the Cox likelihood: \eqn{\mu=\hat\Lambda_0(t)e^{\eta}} (Breslow
estimator), \eqn{w=\mu}, working variate \eqn{\tilde y=\eta+(\delta-\mu)/\mu} with \eqn{\delta}
the event indicator (Ripatti and Palmgren, 2000). The baseline hazard is estimated by Breslow's
method at the last iteration and stored for survival prediction.
}
\examples{
set.seed(1)
data <- simLong(n = 20, p = 6, rel_p = 6, time_points = 10, rho_W = 0.6, rho_Z = 0.6,
                random_sd_intercept = sqrt(0.5),
                random_sd_slope = sqrt(3),
                noise_sd = 0.5, linear = TRUE)  # Generate the data composed by n=20 individuals.
# Train a MEXGB model on the generated data.
mexgb <- MEXGB(X = as.matrix(data[, -1:-5]), Y = as.matrix(data$Y),
               Z = as.matrix(data[, 4:5]), id = data$id, time = data$time,
               ntree = 100, interaction.depth = 3, verbose = TRUE)
mexgb$forest # is the fitted extreme gradient boosting (xgb.Booster) (obtained at the last iteration).
mexgb$random_effects # are the predicted random effects for each individual.
plot(mexgb$LL, type = "o", col = 2) # evolution of the log-likelihood criterion.
mexgb$OOB # training error at each iteration.
summary(mexgb) # relative influence of the predictors.

# Binary outcome
set.seed(1)
datab <- simLong(n = 20, p = 6, rel_p = 6, time_points = 10, linear = FALSE,
                 family = "binomial")
mexgbb <- MEXGB(X = as.matrix(datab[, -1:-5]), Y = datab$Y,
                Z = as.matrix(datab[, 4:5]), id = datab$id, time = datab$time,
                ntree = 100, interaction.depth = 3, family = "binomial")
head(predict(mexgbb, X = as.matrix(datab[, -1:-5]), Z = as.matrix(datab[, 4:5]),
             id = datab$id, time = datab$time))  # predicted probabilities

# Clustered time-to-event outcome (frailty Cox model)
set.seed(1)
datas <- simLong(n = 20, p = 6, time_points = 10, linear = TRUE, family = "cox",
                 random_sd_slope = sqrt(0.5), target_c = 0.75, censoring = 0.3)
Xs <- as.matrix(datas[, grep("^Var", names(datas))])
mexgbs <- MEXGB(X = Xs, Y = cbind(datas$Y, datas$status), Z = as.matrix(datas[, 4:5]),
                id = datas$id, time = datas$time, ntree = 100, interaction.depth = 2,
                family = "cox")
head(predict(mexgbs, X = Xs, Z = as.matrix(datas[, 4:5]), id = datas$id,
             time = datas$time, type = "link"))  # log relative hazards
predict(mexgbs, X = Xs[1:3, ], Z = as.matrix(datas[1:3, 4:5]), id = datas$id[1:3],
        time = datas$time[1:3], type = "survival", times = c(0.5, 1, 2))

}
